{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1157 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "7893530487d3911b2e2d95b929251ad51e57fdce392d3d3f3d4fc3c866cdc10e" :edb/db-before-t 1156 :edb/db-after-t 1157 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534469 50 #inst "2026-10-07T06:39:41.197Z" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064023 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2$, where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$." 13194139534469 false] [17592186064023 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064023 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(2t + 3t^{2})dt \\\\\n&= t^{2} + t^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= (0)^{2} + (0)^{3} + C \\\\\nC &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1\n$$" 13194139534469 false] [17592186064023 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064024 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t$, where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0$." 13194139534469 false] [17592186064024 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064024 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nv(t) &= ∫a(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(1 - 2t)dt \\\\\n&= t - t^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1) = 2$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= (1) - (1)^{2} + C \\\\\nC &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by\n\n$$\nv(t) = t-t^2 + 2\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx(t) &= ∫v(t)dt \\\\\n&= ∫(t - t^{2} + 2)dt \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}t^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}t^{3} + 2t + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1) = 1$. Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= \\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2} - \\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3} + 2(1) + K \\\\\nK &= -\\frac{7}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}\n$$" 13194139534469 false] [17592186064024 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289954 13194139534469 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289964 13194139534469 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289978 13194139534469 true] [17592186064174 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289985 13194139534469 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289957 13194139534469 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289961 13194139534469 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289971 13194139534469 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289992 13194139534469 true] [17592186064175 1020 #edb/ref 17592186289999 13194139534469 true] [17592186064852 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534469 true] [17592186064852 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 6t + 6t^{2}$, where $t > 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x = 2$ when $t = 0$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$." 13194139534469 false] [17592186064852 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186064852 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289955 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1089 "q-351724" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)={csc}^{2}⁡(t),$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=0$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1113 #uuid "d598999c-b003-52da-8100-10acae05a1ed" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289954 1145 "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {csc}^{2}⁡(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+C \\\\ 0 & =-1+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(t)+1.\n$$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289955 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289956 13194139534469 true] [17592186289955 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534469 true] [17592186289955 1041 "field-1" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289955 1104 #edb/ref 17592186289956 13194139534469 true] [17592186289955 1110 #uuid "f6a8c2fc-1baf-5461-8089-f41525dae5c2" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289956 1034 "1-\\cot(t)" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289956 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534469 true] [17592186289956 1111 #uuid "eb7675da-3715-50c7-8e7e-0edb9d8056e9" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1017 #edb/ref 17592186289958 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1089 "q-351730" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1113 #uuid "273d6d87-8669-5f51-96f8-3d528dae9efc" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289957 1145 "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$" 13194139534469 true] [17592186289958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289959 13194139534469 true] [17592186289958 1000 #edb/ref 17592186289960 13194139534469 true] [17592186289958 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534469 true] [17592186289